);
disputeGameFactory.setInitBond(GameTypes.ZK_DISPUTE_GAME, initBond); Old game clones are unaffected. New ZK_DISPUTE_GAME games use zkDisputeGameImpl. The same upgrade MAY disable the source game type by setting its entry to enabled = false with a zero initBond. This requires switching the respected game type in the same upgrade with mechanism 3.1, because OPCM requires the resulting respected game type to be enabled. Disabling clears the source type's implementation and init bond, so no new games of that type can be created. Existing clones keep their implementation and resolve as before. Switch the respected game type to ZK_DISPUTE_GAME with one of: the same upgrade() call, with the extra instruction key overrides.cfg.startingRespectedGameType and data abi.encode(GameTypes.ZK_DISPUTE_GAME). OPCM re-initializes the AnchorStateRegistry with it; or a later Guardian call: anchorStateRegistry.setRespectedGameType(GameTypes.ZK_DISPUTE_GAME); This lets operators register ZK_DISPUTE_GAME first and switch once honest ZK actors are running. Before the switch, an honest ZK proposer, prover and challenger (see Honest Actors ) MUST be operational and funded. This is the chain operator's responsibility and is not enforced by OPCM. Keep the anchor state. upgrade() always re-initializes the AnchorStateRegistry, but it preserves the anchor root, the anchor game, the blacklist and retirementTimestamp. The starting anchor root cannot be overridden during an upgrade. SFDG and SPDG already commit to super roots, the same bytes32 format that ZKDG uses, so the existing anchor is a valid starting point for the first ZKDG game (parentIndex = type(uint32).max). Migrating from a classic output-root FaultDisputeGame with upgrade() is not supported: its anchor is not a super root and cannot be re-seeded during an upgrade.

